module gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_attempt_continuation

// WHAT HAPPENS NEXT ON A NODE'S ATTEMPT LINEAGE. One decision answers "what happens next on this
// attempt" for both the operator's CONTINUE verb and the belt's wake, so a node cannot have two
// answers to one question (gunbc.plans.harness_session_lifecycle, "The decision function"). The
// operator's CONTINUE and START FRESH are the two idempotent verbs; the operator chooses, so a
// lineage is NEVER continued against its evidence. Both verbs share ONE decision fn over a
// PriorExecutionObservation; the only thing the verb changes is the branch the next attempt
// starts from -- CONTINUE the existing branch's head, START FRESH the base ref behind a fresh
// lineage generation (ruling gunbc.design_ruling dispatch_is_continue_or_start_fresh).
//
// WHAT A RESUME IS, IN ONE PLACE: a session that stopped before finishing its task -- a refused
// turn, a stream break, a turn that went silent -- is continued by RESUMING ITS OWN SESSION
// (gunbc.plans.harness_session_lifecycle). This module says WHETHER the prior attempt resumes. It
// never launches anything: the belt actuator (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_belt_actuate) is what carries a
// RESUME into the next attempt's spawn, by the attempt_key the decision names. The session's live
// tmux pane was NEVER an input to this decision, and its absence says nothing about whether the
// lineage has work left. That lane is gone with its turn cap, its turn file and its stretch
// supervisor: a supervised session no longer ends at a round boundary, so whether a lineage
// continues depends on the work it still owes and nothing else (the 0b cut; the supervisor lane's
// consumers are named in the PR that lands this design).
//
// WHY THE STATES COLLAPSED, AND WHY THERE IS NO FENCE HERE (operator ruling, 2026-11-04): the
// pre-redesign decision kept a separate Hold for every way an in-flight lineage can present --
// verification owed, review pending, an auditor running, a session recovery-needed but not yet
// resumable -- and a separate Ended for every way it stopped short. Those are not different
// DECISIONS; they are one decision, "nothing to launch right now, the lineage is still in motion",
// wearing six faces. ContinuationPlan collapses them: a live or in-flight attempt is JOINED
// (AlreadyRunning -- never doubled by a second attempt beside it), and a lineage that stopped short
// of an approved candidate is CONTINUED (NextAttempt -- a new attempt from the existing branch head,
// the work kept), which is exactly what the operator's CONTINUE means -- but ONLY once the prior
// submission capture is SETTLED; a lineage whose capture is still OWED (the belt's commit step has not
// run) is refused and waits for it, never continued onto an unread submission (bright-moth-475: 'owed'
// is not 'handed in nothing'). A fence, a FencedAttempt or a
// per-attempt skip state stays out: the fence was the supervisor's -- it fenced a lane that no longer
// exists -- and a lineage whose session is dead has no live attempt to fence, so the live-attempt
// check IS the double-spawn guard. A session that died is resumed by the decision, not fenced out.

import std.types { Bool, String, Int, List }
import std.algebra { length }
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_refused_retry {
  RefusedRetryStanding, RefusedRetryOwed, RefusedRetryExhausted, RefusedRetryOperatorOnly, RefusedRetryUnreadable, refused_retry_exhausted_reason,
}
import gunbc.harness.harness_session_recovery { SessionRecovery, SessionRecoveryNone, SessionRecoveryPending, SessionRecoveryMalformed, session_resume_bound }
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_submission {
  SubmissionCaptureObservation, CaptureForHead, CaptureAbsent, CaptureUnreadable, CaptureNotAttempted,
  WorkerSubmission, SubmissionReady, SubmissionBlocked, SubmissionNeedsContext,
}
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_workflow_stage {
  WorkflowSegment, WorkflowSegmentState,
  WorkflowSegmentFailed, WorkflowSegmentComplete, WorkflowSegmentPending,
  WorkflowSegmentActive, WorkflowSegmentRefused, WorkflowSegmentStopped,
  WorkflowSegmentKind, VerificationWorkflowSegment, ReviewWorkflowSegment, GoalAuditWorkflowSegment,
}
import gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_workflow_progress {
  AttemptWorktreeObservation, AttemptWorktreePresent, AttemptWorktreeAbsent, AttemptWorktreeUnobserved,
}

// THE PRIOR EXECUTION, EXACTLY AS READ -- not what someone wants it to be. Every member is an
// observation of a past execution the decision is allowed to read, and NOTHING else is. There is no
// live tmux pane among them and there is no turn counter, by construction: the recovery field is
// what the engine's own recorded events say (gunbc.harness.harness_session_recovery), which is where
// the truth about "did that session stop by an unexplained engine refusal, and was it already
// resumed to its bound" actually lives. The worktree, and whether it is reaped with a live branch
// still under it, is read by the belt and consumed by its restore path -- the decision names WHAT
// the next execution is, the belt establishes WHERE it runs.
//
// The node's terminality is NOT a member. A retired lineage is refused by the belt through its one
// node-terminal read (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_node_terminality belt_node_lineage_terminal_read), for
// BOTH verbs and every other verb, not by a member any action path can leave unset (ruling
// gunbc.design_ruling dispatch_is_continue_or_start_fresh).
// THE WORKTREE STANDING IS THE SHARED ATTEMPT-WORKTREE OBSERVER (AttemptWorktreeObservation, from
// gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_workflow_progress) -- there is exactly ONE observer of "does this attempt's worktree
// stand, and is it usable", reused verbatim by the belt's resume arm and by this plan so the two can never
// disagree about the same directory (review 91395, DESIGN §3 nicknaming: a fork is a correctness concern).
// The submission capture and the recovery record live INSIDE the worktree; when it is not present they were NOT
// read, and the plan must not pretend they are 'absent' or 'none' (DESIGN §5: a fabricated default is a hard
// reject). The observer's three states, read not inferred:
//   AttemptWorktreePresent    -- present and usable; the decision never consumes the path, so it is ignored.
//   AttemptWorktreeAbsent     -- its absence is ESTABLISHED from a SUCCEEDED observation (filesystem_io's
//                                absence-adoption rule: never inferred from a failed read). Genuinely gone
//                                (reaped); its branch may still hold the committed work, and the branch
//                                standing decides whether that is a restore.
//   AttemptWorktreeUnobserved -- presence could NOT be established (indeterminate/disagree/refused). Refused,
//                                never restored onto -- restoring over a present-but-unreadable worktree would
//                                clobber or omit uncommitted live work.

// HOW THE PRIOR ATTEMPT'S BRANCH STANDS, AS READ against the shared object store. Consulted only when the
// worktree is absent: a live branch is a restore target (its head is the durable committed work), a gone
// branch leaves nothing to restore, and an unreadable branch is never reported as absent -- a failed check
// is unknown, and unknown is refused. This is what keeps "a failed git command" from being read as "the
// branch is gone".
type BranchStanding
  = BranchLive
  | BranchGone
  | BranchUnknown { reason: String }

// HOW THE PRIOR ATTEMPT'S WORKTREE STANDS -- and ONLY the facts that could be read given that standing.
// This is a SUM, not a flat record, on purpose (DESIGN 5: "stronger than catching a wrong state is making
// it unwritable -- correctness by construction, not validation"). The submission capture and the recovery
// record live INSIDE the worktree, so they can be read ONLY while it is present; the durable branch standing
// is the substitute ONLY once it is reaped. A flat record let the belt pair a reaped worktree with a
// FABRICATED placeholder capture (DESIGN 5: "a fabricated default ... is a hard reject") and let a consumer
// read a field it never populated, treating a placeholder as a real reading. Each fact now exists only in the
// arm that could have read it, so an impossible pairing (a reaped tree WITH a real capture; a present tree
// WITH a branch standing consulted) is unwritable rather than ruled out by convention and match ORDER alone.
type WorktreeStanding
  // The worktree is HERE: the capture and recovery that live inside it WERE read. The branch standing is
  // deliberately absent here -- while the tree stands there is nothing to restore onto, so it is never consulted.
  = WorktreeStands { path: String, capture: SubmissionCaptureObservation, recovery: SessionRecovery }
  // The worktree is GONE -- established by a listing that SUCCEEDED and found it absent (a failed read is
  // Unobserved below, never this). The capture/recovery that lived inside it were NOT read and have no field
  // to be faked into; the durable fact is the BRANCH, read against the shared object store that survives
  // reaping, and THAT is what the decision routes on (lab bug #2: a reaped worktree must not block the decision).
  | WorktreeReaped { path: String, branch_head: BranchStanding }
  // Presence could NOT be established -- the root was unlistable, the subject refused, or a live tree's head
  // could not be read. Neither the capture nor the branch is trusted; the decision refuses, never restores onto
  // a possibly-live directory (DESIGN 5 filesystem_io: absence is established only by a listing that succeeded).
  | WorktreeUnknown { path: String, cause: String }

type PriorExecutionObservation = {
  attempt_key: String,
  branch: String,
  worker_terminal: Bool,
  standing: WorktreeStanding,
  // THE LEDGER SEGMENTS, read flat -- these survive a worktree reap (they live in the ledger, not the tree),
  // so they are consulted on BOTH the present and reaped paths and are genuinely independent of the standing.
  verification: WorkflowSegment?,
  review: WorkflowSegment?,
  goal_audit: WorkflowSegment?,
  // WHERE THE REFUSED VERIFICATION STANDS against the belt's retry bound (gunbc.roadmap.roadmap_refused_retry),
  // read from the receipt's typed cause and the ledger beside it; consulted only on a refused verification segment.
  verification_retry: RefusedRetryStanding,
}

// WHAT TO DO WITH THE NODE'S WORK NEXT. The seven variants are a partition of what a prior execution
// can mean for what comes next; there is deliberately no "wait" variant -- waiting is AlreadyRunning,
// because an in-flight lineage is joined, not waited on by a fresh spawn. The plan says what the next
// execution IS; it never launches, never writes the ledger, and never touches a worktree itself.
//   StartInitial        -- no prior execution at all: this is the first attempt, taken from the base ref.
//   AlreadyRunning      -- an attempt is live, or the lineage is still in motion (a segment owed,
//                          pending, active or retried-on-environment): JOIN it; a second attempt is
//                          never spawned beside it. Nothing launches. Carries the attempt to join.
//   Resume              -- the prior worker is terminal, handed in nothing, and its session stopped
//                          by an unexplained engine refusal that it has not yet answered and is within
//                          the declared resume bound: continue that attempt's OWN session from the
//                          validated point its ordinal names, in the same worktree and conversation.
//   NextAttempt         -- the lineage stopped short of an approved candidate (a judged verdict, a
//                          blocked or needs-context submission, a handed-in nothing with no recovery,
//                          a session flapped past its bound): CONTINUE by starting a NEW attempt from
//                          the existing branch's head -- the work is kept, never started over. Carries
//                          the branch its new attempt starts from.
//   Restore             -- CONTINUE's recovery of a STILL-RESUMABLE attempt whose worktree was reaped while
//                          its lineage had NOT ended: rebuild the SAME attempt in place (same attempt key)
//                          from its live branch and reopen the claim that names it. NOT NextAttempt (which
//                          mints a NEW key for a lineage that ENDED without a candidate). A live branch is
//                          required; a gone/unknown branch or an unreadable tree is a Refuse, never a restore.
//   AlreadyComplete     -- verification green, review approved and goal audit approved: the lineage is
//                          complete; the operator's CONTINUE is a no-op and NOTHING spawns.
//   Refuse              -- a fact the decision needs cannot be established at all: the ledger is
//                          unobservable, a segment sits in a state it does not inhabit, a capture is
//                          unreadable, a recovery record is malformed. Carries the located reason. A
//                          refusal is never a fallback into a spawn.
type ContinuationPlan
  = StartInitial { reason: String }
  | AlreadyRunning { reason: String, attempt_key: String }
  | Resume { reason: String, attempt_key: String, ordinal: Int, causes: List<String> }
  | NextAttempt { reason: String, from_branch: String }
  // Restore -- CONTINUE's recovery of a STILL-RESUMABLE attempt whose worktree was reaped while its
  // lineage had NOT ended: the belt rebuilds the SAME attempt in place (same attempt key) from its live
  // branch and reopens the claim that names it (worktree_claim_reopen_settled on the same CAS slot). It is
  // NOT NextAttempt (which mints a NEW key for a lineage that ENDED without a candidate); a live attempt is
  // recovered under its own key so its ledger history is kept, never abandoned to an orphan (operator ruling
  // D3). Its branch must be live: a gone branch is nothing to restore onto (Refuse), an unreadable branch is
  // unknown and refused, never restored onto. It carries the key it restores and the branch it restores from.
  | Restore { reason: String, attempt_key: String, from_branch: String }
  | AlreadyComplete { reason: String }
  // Refuse carries BOTH a typed cause (so a consumer matches the DISTINCTION without parsing prose -- DESIGN
  // §4c "belongs in a typed carrier", §5 "fails with a typed, located diagnostic") AND a human-readable reason
  // (the belt shows it verbatim; it is a rendering, never the discriminator). The cause is the class; the
  // reason is its sentence. Before this, each cause travelled as an in-band "BranchAbsent: …" text tag inside
  // a bare String, and witnesses grep'd that prose to recover a distinction the model should have carried
  // (review 91440; §5 "a check restating a constraint the model already carries is a second representation").
  | Refuse { cause: RefuseCause, reason: String }

// THE TYPED CAUSE of a Refuse: the distinct reasons the decision already tells apart, each its own variant so
// the witnesses and the belt match on shape, never on a substring of prose. BranchAbsent is the operator's
// "typed BranchAbsent refusal"; the detail-carrying variants keep their located diagnostic in-field.
type RefuseCause
  = BranchAbsent
  | BranchUndetermined { reason: String }
  | WorktreeUnobserved { cause: String }
  | CaptureStoreUnreadable { reason: String }
  | CaptureOwed { reason: String }
  // SegmentDamaged -- a ledger segment sits in WorkflowSegmentStopped, a state that segment does not inhabit
  // (only the agent segment has a round budget to stop on). The WHICH-SEGMENT distinction is carried by the
  // REUSED WorkflowSegmentKind coproduct (DESIGN 4c: a closed set belongs in a typed carrier, not a String;
  // DESIGN 2/3: reuse the existing segment identity rather than mint a parallel SegmentKind).
  | SegmentDamaged { segment: WorkflowSegmentKind }
  | LedgerUnobservable { reason: String }
  // RetryExhausted / RetryLedgerUnreadable -- the belt's bounded retry of an infrastructure-refused verification
  // (ruling infrastructure_refusal_is_retried_to_a_bound): the bound is spent, or the belt's own ledger could not be read.
  | RetryExhausted { attempts: Int }
  | RetryLedgerUnreadable { reason: String }
  // RecoveryRecordMalformed -- the prior attempt's recovery record exists but is unreadable (its recorded
  // fields do not line up), so neither a resume nor a next attempt is established for a worker that handed
  // in nothing. Distinct from an unreadable SUBMISSION STORE (CaptureStoreUnreadable): this is the recovery
  // carrier, read alongside the capture on the present-worktree path.
  | RecoveryRecordMalformed { mismatch: String }

// THE ONE DECISION FUNCTION, shared by the operator's CONTINUE verb and the belt's wake.
// It reads ONLY the prior execution observation, never a desired state. No prior at all is the
// first attempt; otherwise the prior's own facts decide, exactly as below.
fn continuation_plan(obs: PriorExecutionObservation?) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match obs {
    Absent =>
      StartInitial {
        reason: "this node has no prior attempt on the ledger, so this is the first attempt, taken from the base ref",
      }
    Present { value: prior } =>
      if !prior.worker_terminal {
        // A LIVE WORKER IS JOINED -- but only while its worktree is still HERE. A live (not-terminal) worker
        // whose worktree was REAPED is the accident M3 prevents: its session is resumable, so its claim should
        // never have been released and its tree reclaimed. When that has already happened, CONTINUE neither
        // JOINS a session whose tree is gone nor starts a NEW attempt (the lineage has not ENDED); it RESTORES
        // the SAME attempt in place from its live branch and reopens the claim naming it (operator ruling D3).
        // The double-spawn guard still holds: no second attempt key is ever minted for a live attempt -- a
        // present worktree JOINs under its key, a reaped worktree RESTORES the same key, nothing doubles it.
        match prior.standing {
          WorktreeStands { path: _, capture: _, recovery: _ } =>
            AlreadyRunning {
              reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " is live: its worker is not terminal; its session is JOINED and no second attempt is started beside it"], ""),
              attempt_key: prior.attempt_key,
            }
          WorktreeReaped { path: _, branch_head } =>
            match branch_head {
              BranchLive =>
                Restore {
                  reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " is live (not terminal) but its worktree was reaped; its session is resumable, so the SAME attempt is restored in place from its live branch ", prior.branch, " and its claim reopened -- no new attempt key is minted"], ""),
                  attempt_key: prior.attempt_key,
                  from_branch: prior.branch,
                }
              BranchGone =>
                Refuse { cause: BranchAbsent, reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " is live but its worktree is reaped and its branch ", prior.branch, " is gone, so there is nothing to restore the resumable attempt onto"], "") }
              BranchUnknown { reason: why } =>
                Refuse { cause: BranchUndetermined { reason: why }, reason: join(["could not determine whether the live attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s branch ", prior.branch, " is present to restore its reaped worktree from; a failed branch check is not an absence (", why, ")"], "") }
            }
          WorktreeUnknown { path: _, cause } =>
            Refuse { cause: WorktreeUnobserved { cause }, reason: join(["the worktree standing of the live attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " could not be established (", cause, "), so it is neither joined nor restored onto a possibly-absent tree"], "") }
        }
      } else {
        // THE WORKTREE STANDING IS READ FIRST, because the capture and recovery live INSIDE it. When the
        // worktree is absent they were NOT read, so the plan decides on the BRANCH standing (read against
        // the shared object store, which survives reaping) instead of pretending the capture is 'absent'.
        match prior.standing {
          WorktreeReaped { path: _, branch_head } =>
            // LAB BUG #2 (a reaped worktree must not block the decision): the worktree is gone -- reaped
            // after the work was committed -- but the durable fact is the BRANCH. The capture was NOT read
            // (it lived inside the gone worktree); the decision reads the LEDGER instead, which survives the
            // reap, and only then considers the branch (review 91383). A live branch is CONTINUE's general
            // restore target -- but ONLY for a lineage that ended without an approved candidate; a lineage
            // whose ledger already says complete is AlreadyComplete (its CONTINUE is a no-op, nothing spawns)
            // and one with a verdict still in motion is JOINED, never doubled by a spawn beside it. A gone
            // branch leaves nothing; an unreadable branch is unknown, and unknown is refused, never restored
            // onto (a failed check is not the fact that a branch is gone).
            match branch_head {
              BranchLive => continuation_from_segments(prior: prior)
              BranchGone =>
                Refuse { cause: BranchAbsent, reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s branch ", prior.branch, " is gone and its worktree is not present, so there is no committed work to continue and nothing to restore onto"], "") }
              BranchUnknown { reason: why } =>
                Refuse { cause: BranchUndetermined { reason: why }, reason: join(["could not determine whether the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s branch ", prior.branch, " is present; a failed branch check is not an absent branch and is never restored onto: ", why], "") }
            }
          WorktreeUnknown { path: _, cause } =>
            // PRESENCE COULD NOT BE ESTABLISHED -- the root was unlistable, the subject refused, or the
            // directory lists but its head could not be read (a git fault on a LIVE worktree). This is not
            // the reaped case: it must never be restored onto, because a still-present worktree may hold
            // uncommitted live work that a restore-from-branch would clobber or silently omit. A read that
            // failed is unknown, and unknown is refused (DESIGN §5: runtime uncertainty -> typed refusal;
            // filesystem_io: absence is established only by a listing that succeeded). The belt's own
            // re-observe is the remedy, not a guessed spawn.
            Refuse { cause: WorktreeUnobserved { cause }, reason: join(["could not establish how the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s worktree stands, so the lineage decides nothing rather than restoring onto a possibly-live directory: ", cause], "") }
          WorktreeStands { path: _, capture, recovery } =>
        match capture {
          CaptureUnreadable { reason } =>
            Refuse { cause: CaptureStoreUnreadable { reason }, reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s submission store is unreadable, so what it holds is unknown and the lineage decides nothing: ", reason], "") }
          CaptureAbsent =>
            continuation_from_absent_capture(prior: prior, recovery: recovery)
          // THE CAPTURE IS OWED -- do NOT continue, and do NOT relabel the obligation as handed-in-nothing.
          // CaptureNotAttempted is produced only when the belt's commit step has not SETTLED this head (no
          // commit record, or one at another head / deferred / failed): every other consumer in the repo
          // reads it as owed (roadmap_dogfood_route StageOwed, roadmap_belt_actuate ClaimCaptureOwed), and
          // the recurring-failure-mode record absent_reads_identically_to_never_looked is explicit that no
          // capture because-it-is-owed and no capture-because-it-was-settled return the same listing -- so
          // the decision must NOT decide. Starting a new attempt from the branch head here would silently
          // drop work the prior worker had not yet committed. The bounded, not-forever nature of this wait
          // is held by the belt's commit lane itself (belt_capture_absence_for_attempt reads the producer's
          // own commit record and settles it), not by inventing a 'legacy' reading the observation cannot
          // support: nothing in this observation can tell an owed-now capture from an old one, so the
          // decision refuses and waits rather than fabricating the distinction (DESIGN §5).
          CaptureNotAttempted { reason } =>
            Refuse { cause: CaptureOwed { reason }, reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " has ended but its submission capture is still owed (", reason, "), so whether its branch holds a declared candidate has not been read; the belt's commit lane settles it, and CONTINUE waits for that capture rather than starting a new attempt from a branch head that would omit work the prior worker had not committed"], "") }
          CaptureForHead { capture: c } =>
            match c.submission.readiness {
              SubmissionBlocked =>
                continuation_next_attempt(prior: prior, why: join(["its worker reported it was blocked: ", continuation_bounded_detail(detail: c.submission.explanation)], ""))
              SubmissionNeedsContext =>
                continuation_next_attempt(prior: prior, why: join(["its submission declared needs-context: ", continuation_bounded_detail(detail: c.submission.explanation)], ""))
              SubmissionReady =>
                continuation_from_segments(prior: prior)
            }
        }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE ONLY PLACE THE DECIDE-WHO-CONTINUES RULE LIVES. A WORKER THAT HANDED IN NOTHING has three
// distinct outcomes, kept distinct on purpose: (1) its session stopped at a validated state by an
// unexplained engine refusal it has not yet answered and is within the declared bound -> RESUME its
// own session from the validated point, whose resume number is `ordinal` (the next resume, bounded at
// the session's declared bound); (2) the session is recovery-needed but NOT resumable (an interrupted tool call
// whose effect is not established) -> JOIN, the effect's outcome is to be established first, nothing
// launches; (3) past the bound -> CONTINUE with a new attempt from the branch head, naming every
// recorded cause so a flapping engine is visible and bounded, never absorbed. No recovery recorded
// -- a refusal that explained itself, a hosted route, a worker that simply stopped -- CONTINUES with
// a new attempt from the existing branch head. The recovery record is read through
// gunbc.harness.harness_session_recovery, the one reader (gunbc.rung_drop
// unobservable_tmux_pane_liveness is unchanged by this: no recovery record is a recovery-NEEDED
// session, and that record is read directly, never through the pane).
fn continuation_from_absent_capture(prior: PriorExecutionObservation, recovery: SessionRecovery) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match recovery {
    SessionRecoveryNone =>
      continuation_next_attempt(prior: prior, why: "its worker ended without handing in a candidate; the attempt's event stream records how the session ended, and the new attempt starts from the existing branch head so any committed work is kept")
    SessionRecoveryMalformed { mismatch: mismatch } =>
      Refuse {
        cause: RecoveryRecordMalformed { mismatch },
        reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s recovery record is unreadable, so neither a resume nor a next attempt is established: ", mismatch], ""),
      }
    SessionRecoveryPending { causes: causes, resumes_made: made, interrupted_calls: interrupted, resume_round: _ } =>
      if length(xs: interrupted) > 0 {
        AlreadyRunning {
          reason: join([
            "the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " is recovery-needed but not resumable: tool calls ", join(interrupted, ", "),
            " have no recorded result, so their effects are not established; the last cause was ", join(causes, " | "),
          ], ""),
          attempt_key: prior.attempt_key,
        }
      } else if made >= session_resume_bound {
        continuation_next_attempt(prior: prior, why: join([
          "its session was resumed ", to_string(value: made), " times, the bound of ", to_string(value: session_resume_bound),
          ", and was refused again; every recorded cause: ", join(causes, " | "),
        ], ""))
      } else {
        Resume {
          reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " stopped at a validated state and is resumed in its own session, resume ", to_string(value: made + 1), " of ", to_string(value: session_resume_bound), "; cause: ", join(causes, " | ")], ""),
          attempt_key: prior.attempt_key,
          ordinal: made + 1,
          causes: causes,
        }
      }
  }
}

// A LINEAGE THAT STOPPED SHORT OF AN APPROVED CANDIDATE IS CONTINUED, NOT ENDED. The next attempt
// starts from the EXISTING branch's head so the work the prior attempt committed stays in the new
// attempt's base -- CONTINUE keeps the work; only START FRESH abandons it behind a new lineage
// generation. The branch is carried so the witness pins where the new attempt starts, and the
// reason carries the located judgement that stopped the lineage short.
fn continuation_next_attempt(prior: PriorExecutionObservation, why: String) -> ContinuationPlan {
  NextAttempt {
    reason: join([
      "the attempt lineage at ", prior.attempt_key, " stopped short of an approved candidate: ", why,
      " -- its work stays on branch ", prior.branch, ", and the next attempt starts from that branch's head",
    ], ""),
    from_branch: prior.branch,
  }
}

// THE REAPED-MODE TERMINAL of the single shared ledger walk (continuation_from_segments with reaped=true):
// when the ledger shows the lineage ended WITHOUT an approved candidate -- a failed verdict, or a segment the
// belt never got to record -- CONTINUE names a fresh attempt that starts from the still-live branch head: the
// belt re-creates (restores) the reaped worktree from that head (the capture inside the gone worktree was never
// read; the branch carries the work). LAB BUG #2: after a reap the belt can only act by restoring, so joining
// forever on an owed segment would itself be the block the bug forbids. The complete / in-flight / damaged
// outcomes are NOT here -- they are the SHARED cascade arms (AlreadyComplete / AlreadyRunning / Refuse), so a
// change to segment semantics is made in ONE place (review 91390, DESIGN §6b). THIS IS THE NEXT-ATTEMPT (NEW
// KEY) REAPED PATH: it mints a fresh attempt off the branch for a lineage that ENDED without a candidate. It is
// DISTINCT from the same-key ContinuationPlan.Restore, which recovers a STILL-LIVE attempt under its own key.
fn continuation_reaped_next_attempt(prior: PriorExecutionObservation, why: String) -> ContinuationPlan {
  continuation_next_attempt(
    prior: prior,
    why: join([why, "; its worktree is gone but its branch ", prior.branch, " is still live, so the committed work is the durable record -- a NEW attempt (a fresh key, NOT the same-key Restore which recovers a still-live attempt) is started off that branch's head"], ""),
  )
}

// A WORKER-AUTHORED EXPLANATION IS UNBOUNDED TEXT, and it lands in a reason the dashboard shows; the
// plan carries at most this much of it. The truncation is marked so a cut explanation is never
// mistaken for a whole one.
data continuation_verdict_detail_bound: Int = 400

fn continuation_bounded_detail(detail: String) -> String {
  if string_length(s: detail) <= continuation_verdict_detail_bound {
    detail
  } else {
    concat(substring(s: detail, start: 0, end: continuation_verdict_detail_bound), " ...")
  }
}

// THE WORKFLOW DECIDES. The pipeline is the spine, the engine is the exception, and a verdict the
// worker's own hand did not produce outranks it. A segment that is owed, pending, active, or refused
// on an environment fact is a lineage still in MOTION: it is JOINED, never doubled by a spawn, and
// the belt owes the segment itself, not a fresh attempt. A FAILED segment is the located judgement
// that stopped the lineage short, so it CONTINUES with a new attempt from the branch head -- CONTINUE
// keeps the work and lets the worker try again on top of it, which is the whole point of the verb.
// WHAT THE WALK DOES WHEN A SEGMENT IS ABSENT (its belt obligation was never recorded). The branch is read
// from the standing the caller already matched -- there is NO reaped: Bool to lie with, so a present-worktree
// caller cannot claim a reap (DESIGN 5: a check restating a constraint the model carries is a second
// representation of it; here the standing is the ONE source). After a REAP the belt can only act by restoring
// the worktree from the branch head, so it continues with a fresh attempt off the branch; on a LIVE tree the
// segment is merely OWED, so the lineage is JOINED and the belt performs the obligation, nothing spawning.
// WorktreeUnknown cannot reach the walk (presence is refused upstream); it is matched for closedness (4b) and
// fails typed rather than guessed.
fn continuation_segment_absent(prior: PriorExecutionObservation, why: String, join_reason: String) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match prior.standing {
    WorktreeReaped { path: _, branch_head: _ } =>
      continuation_reaped_next_attempt(prior: prior, why: why)
    WorktreeStands { path: _, capture: _, recovery: _ } =>
      AlreadyRunning { reason: join_reason, attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
    WorktreeUnknown { path: _, cause } =>
      Refuse { cause: WorktreeUnobserved { cause }, reason: "the segment walk is reached only once the worktree's standing is established; an unobservable worktree is refused upstream, so this arm is unreachable and fails typed rather than guessed" }
  }
}

// WHAT THE WALK DOES WHEN A SEGMENT FAILED (a red verdict): a REAP restores a fresh attempt from the branch
// head; a LIVE tree continues the lineage with a new attempt on the branch. Typed on the standing, not a flag.
fn continuation_segment_failed(prior: PriorExecutionObservation, why: String) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match prior.standing {
    WorktreeReaped { path: _, branch_head: _ } =>
      continuation_reaped_next_attempt(prior: prior, why: why)
    WorktreeStands { path: _, capture: _, recovery: _ } =>
      continuation_next_attempt(prior: prior, why: why)
    WorktreeUnknown { path: _, cause } =>
      Refuse { cause: WorktreeUnobserved { cause }, reason: "unreachable -- the segment walk is reached only with an established standing; see continuation_segment_absent" }
  }
}

// A REFUSED VERIFICATION IS AN ENVIRONMENT FACT, not a verdict. While the bound leaves a retry the lineage is
// JOINED (the belt re-runs the stage on its next wake); a spent bound or an unreadable ledger refuses with a
// typed cause; a refusal about the candidate (or no receipt cause) is the operator's, joined and not retried.
fn continuation_refused_verification(prior: PriorExecutionObservation) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match prior.verification_retry {
    RefusedRetryOwed { ordinal } =>
      AlreadyRunning {
        reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s verification was refused by its infrastructure, an environment fact rather than a verdict: the belt re-runs it on its next wake (retry ", to_string(ordinal), " of its bound), so the lineage is joined"], ""),
        attempt_key: prior.attempt_key,
      }
    RefusedRetryExhausted { attempts } =>
      Refuse { cause: RetryExhausted { attempts }, reason: refused_retry_exhausted_reason(attempts: attempts) }
    RefusedRetryUnreadable { reason } =>
      Refuse { cause: RetryLedgerUnreadable { reason }, reason: join(["the belt could not establish the refused verification's retry standing (its receipt, its typed cause or its retry ledger), so it cannot say whether a retry is owed; the line stops and the operator decides: ", reason], "") }
    RefusedRetryOperatorOnly =>
      AlreadyRunning {
        reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s verification was refused for a reason about the candidate, not its environment, so the belt does not re-run it on its own: the line stops here for the attempt and the operator decides, so the lineage is joined"], ""),
        attempt_key: prior.attempt_key,
      }
  }
}

fn continuation_from_segments(prior: PriorExecutionObservation) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match prior.verification {
    Absent =>
      continuation_segment_absent(
        prior: prior,
        why: "no verification segment was ever recorded, and after a reap the belt can only act by restoring the worktree",
        join_reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s verification is owed and has no segment yet; the belt owes it, so the lineage is joined and nothing spawns"], ""),
      )
    Present { value: v } =>
      match v.state {
        WorkflowSegmentFailed =>
          continuation_segment_failed(prior: prior, why: join(["verification failed: ", v.detail], ""))
        WorkflowSegmentComplete => continuation_after_green_verification(prior: prior)
        WorkflowSegmentStopped => Refuse { cause: SegmentDamaged { segment: VerificationWorkflowSegment }, reason: "the prior attempt's verification segment is stopped, a state verification does not inhabit -- the segment record is damaged and the operator decides" }
        WorkflowSegmentPending =>
          AlreadyRunning {
            reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, "'s verification is owed and still pending; the belt owes it, so the lineage is joined and nothing spawns"], ""),
            attempt_key: prior.attempt_key,
          }
        WorkflowSegmentActive =>
          AlreadyRunning { reason: "the prior attempt's verification is running; its result is awaited, and no attempt spawns beside it", attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
        WorkflowSegmentRefused => continuation_refused_verification(prior: prior)
      }
  }
}

// A GREEN VERIFICATION HANDS THE QUESTION TO THE REVIEW: changes requested stop the lineage short and
// CONTINUE with a new attempt on the branch, an approved review hands it to the goal audit, anything
// else is a lineage still in motion and is joined.
fn continuation_after_green_verification(prior: PriorExecutionObservation) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match prior.review {
    Absent =>
      continuation_segment_absent(
        prior: prior,
        why: "its verification was green but it carries no review segment, and after a reap the belt can only act by restoring the worktree",
        join_reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " verified green and carries no review segment yet; review is owed, so the lineage is joined"], ""),
      )
    Present { value: r } =>
      match r.state {
        WorkflowSegmentFailed =>
          continuation_segment_failed(prior: prior, why: join(["review requested changes: ", r.detail], ""))
        WorkflowSegmentComplete => continuation_after_approved_review(prior: prior)
        WorkflowSegmentPending =>
          AlreadyRunning { reason: join(["the prior attempt verified green and awaits review: ", r.detail], ""), attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
        WorkflowSegmentActive =>
          AlreadyRunning { reason: "the prior attempt verified green and its reviewer is running; its result is awaited, and no attempt spawns beside it", attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
        WorkflowSegmentRefused =>
          AlreadyRunning { reason: join(["the prior attempt verified green and its review refused: ", r.detail], ""), attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
        WorkflowSegmentStopped => Refuse { cause: SegmentDamaged { segment: ReviewWorkflowSegment }, reason: "the prior attempt's review segment is stopped, a state review does not inhabit -- the segment record is damaged and the operator decides" }
      }
  }
}

// AN APPROVED REVIEW HANDS THE QUESTION TO THE GOAL AUDIT, the same way: changes requested stop the
// lineage short and CONTINUE with a new attempt on the branch, approval COMPLETES it, and anything
// else is a lineage still in motion and is joined.
fn continuation_after_approved_review(prior: PriorExecutionObservation) -> ContinuationPlan {
  match prior.goal_audit {
    Absent =>
      continuation_segment_absent(
        prior: prior,
        why: "its review approved but it carries no goal-audit segment, and after a reap the belt can only act by restoring the worktree",
        join_reason: join(["the prior attempt ", prior.attempt_key, " verified green and its review approved, but it carries no goal audit segment yet; the audit is owed, so the lineage is joined"], ""),
      )
    Present { value: a } =>
      match a.state {
        WorkflowSegmentFailed =>
          continuation_segment_failed(prior: prior, why: join(["goal audit requested changes: ", a.detail], ""))
        WorkflowSegmentComplete => AlreadyComplete { reason: "the prior attempt verified green, its review approved and its goal audit approved; the lineage is complete and the operator's CONTINUE is a no-op -- nothing spawns" }
        WorkflowSegmentPending =>
          AlreadyRunning { reason: join(["the prior attempt awaits its goal audit: ", a.detail], ""), attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
        WorkflowSegmentActive =>
          AlreadyRunning { reason: "the prior attempt's goal auditor is running; its result is awaited, and no attempt spawns beside it", attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
        WorkflowSegmentRefused =>
          AlreadyRunning { reason: join(["the prior attempt's goal audit refused: ", a.detail], ""), attempt_key: prior.attempt_key }
        WorkflowSegmentStopped => Refuse { cause: SegmentDamaged { segment: GoalAuditWorkflowSegment }, reason: "the prior attempt's goal audit segment is stopped, a state the audit does not inhabit -- the segment record is damaged and the operator decides" }
      }
  }
}
